WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.764 --> 00:00:17.532
My name is Brother John, and I am a monk at Croyland Abbey, a monastery on the east

2
00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:18.432
coast of England.

3
00:00:19.393 --> 00:00:25.916
This monastery is a religious community of men who live together in seclusion and raise their own food.

4
00:00:26.777 --> 00:00:33.653
Croyland Abbey is self-sustaining, so monks rarely need to leave the monastery, and most never do.

5
00:00:34.653 --> 00:00:39.035
Since this is a Benedictine abbey, we live according to the Rule of St.

6
00:00:39.455 --> 00:00:43.537
Benedict, a set of rules created long ago by St.

7
00:00:43.617 --> 00:00:50.820
Benedict. He was an Italian monk who started a monastery about six hundred years ago, just south of

8
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:57.883
Rome. We spend our days praying, praising God, and working at various jobs in the monastery.

9
00:00:58.703 --> 00:01:01.064
Most of the time, we do not speak.

10
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:06.664
The year is 1150, and I have been here for twenty years.

11
00:01:08.005 --> 00:01:13.770
I am the youngest son of a wheat farmer, and by law, only the oldest son may inherit

12
00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:14.731
the family farm.

13
00:01:15.632 --> 00:01:22.177
This law makes it very difficult for younger sons to make a living, so my future was very

14
00:01:22.317 --> 00:01:29.683
uncertain. This worried my father, so when I was seven years old, my father decided to give me

15
00:01:29.743 --> 00:01:31.704
to the monks at Croyland Abbey.

16
00:01:32.665 --> 00:01:39.807
My mother cried, and so did I, but my father said the monks would educate me, and I

17
00:01:39.847 --> 00:01:41.847
would learn a trade or profession.

18
00:01:42.507 --> 00:01:48.389
He thought I'd have a better chance at a meaningful life, and did not want me to end

19
00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:51.370
up as a menial laborer on my brother's farm.

20
00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.071
My mother finally agreed to let me go.

21
00:01:56.331 --> 00:02:02.390
For the next five years, I worked in the monastery kitchen and tended the sheep in the pastures.

22
00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:09.755
Every afternoon a monk taught me and the other boys how to read and write using the Holy

23
00:02:09.815 --> 00:02:14.638
Scriptures. We attended chapel, but not as much as the monks.

24
00:02:15.339 --> 00:02:21.903
The monastery cook noticed that I had an interest in herbs because I spent all of my spare

25
00:02:21.963 --> 00:02:22.924
time in the garden.

26
00:02:23.844 --> 00:02:29.564
He thought I might make a good herbalist some day, and that made me happy, since I had

27
00:02:29.644 --> 00:02:33.346
grown to love the quiet of the monastery gardens.

28
00:02:34.547 --> 00:02:40.870
At the age of 12, I chose to become a novice, which is the first step to becoming

29
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.252
a real religious member of a monastery.

30
00:02:44.072 --> 00:02:50.616
During a special ceremony, an older monk shaved a bald patch on the top of my head, which

31
00:02:50.716 --> 00:02:53.397
signified that I was a servant of God.

32
00:02:55.198 --> 00:02:58.000
Then I made a vow to follow the Rule of St.

33
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:05.323
Benedict, obey God and the monks, cherish silence, and fulfill all my duties to the monastery.

34
00:03:06.384 --> 00:03:12.047
When I was 15 years old, I took my final vows to become a monk, and I promised

35
00:03:12.407 --> 00:03:13.328
never to marry.

36
00:03:14.028 --> 00:03:20.271
I also gave up everything I owned and promised to obey the abbot, the head monk.

37
00:03:21.292 --> 00:03:24.874
There was no turning back, and I would be a monk for life.

38
00:03:26.455 --> 00:03:32.972
Now I work in the herb garden, where I grow many different kinds of plants to make medicine

39
00:03:33.392 --> 00:03:37.216
for the other monks or for the village people seeking help.

40
00:03:37.977 --> 00:03:39.078
Another rule of St.

41
00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:42.741
Benedict states that the sick should never be forgotten.

42
00:03:43.502 --> 00:03:49.948
Of course monks know that human life is in God's hands, but plants are made by God too,

43
00:03:50.508 --> 00:03:54.052
and we believe they should be used to ease human suffering.

44
00:03:55.744 --> 00:04:02.307
To educate myself I spend many hours reading books by the ancient Greeks and by a Persian named

45
00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:09.610
Avicenna. These books have important information about hundreds of plants and the diseases they cure.

46
00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:15.792
They contain instructions on how to grow the plants, and recipes for how to prepare them for certain

47
00:04:15.852 --> 00:04:21.675
maladies. For example, mint is very common in the meadows around the monastery.

48
00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:27.907
When I add mint leaves to boiling water, I can make a tea that cures a stomachache!

49
00:04:28.487 --> 00:04:34.332
Any part of a plant can be used to make medicine, but I usually use the roots, leaves,

50
00:04:34.733 --> 00:04:35.453
and seeds.

51
00:04:36.274 --> 00:04:42.260
I collect the plants from the garden and allow them to dry out; then I crush them into

52
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.744
powder so they mix easily with oil, water, honey, or vinegar.

53
00:04:48.725 --> 00:04:55.109
Many times I put the powders I make into small bags and place them on a patient's skin

54
00:04:55.189 --> 00:04:56.749
to ease soreness.

55
00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:04.692
A powder made from a yellow flower called feverfew, when mixed with honey, can be taken by mouth

56
00:05:04.752 --> 00:05:07.312
to calm a cough and reduce a fever.

57
00:05:07.793 --> 00:05:14.194
If mixed with water and rubbed on the skin, feverfew also stops the itch of an insect bite.

58
00:05:15.755 --> 00:05:19.536
When I became a monk, I was given a plain robe with a hood.

59
00:05:20.296 --> 00:05:25.300
Sometimes I cover my head with the hood when I am in deep thought and do not want

60
00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:26.200
to talk to anyone.

61
00:05:26.781 --> 00:05:33.245
Silence is so important to everyone here at Croyland Abbey that monks use hand signals in order not

62
00:05:33.385 --> 00:05:34.786
to disturb the stillness.

63
00:05:35.307 --> 00:05:39.990
We have more than one hundred signals that we use at mealtimes alone!

64
00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:46.374
For instance, if I want someone to pass me a plate of fish at dinner, I make a

65
00:05:46.975 --> 00:05:48.576
swimming motion with my hands.

66
00:05:49.732 --> 00:05:50.233
One of St.

67
00:05:50.293 --> 00:05:57.219
Benedict's rules says that a monk must be ready at all times to serve God, so we sleep

68
00:05:57.259 --> 00:05:58.680
in our clothes every night.

69
00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:05.826
Then we awake very early and rush to the chapel to praise God with songs and prayers.

70
00:06:06.347 --> 00:06:12.492
We also have a special prayer book, called a book of hours, which we carry to prayer eight

71
00:06:12.592 --> 00:06:13.453
times a day.

72
00:06:14.093 --> 00:06:19.253
When the bell sounds, it means it is time for prayer, and we must stop whatever we are

73
00:06:19.293 --> 00:06:21.934
doing and go to the chapel immediately.

74
00:06:22.814 --> 00:06:28.957
We first pray before sunrise, and the final call to prayer is just before bedtime.

75
00:06:29.677 --> 00:06:35.219
Luckily the gardens are not far from the chapel, so it is easy for me to get there

76
00:06:35.559 --> 00:06:36.740
without too much trouble.

77
00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.661
I love being a monk and a herbalist.

78
00:06:40.401 --> 00:06:45.784
The years of working in the garden and helping my patients have taught me much about the world

79
00:06:45.944 --> 00:06:46.564
and God.

80
00:06:47.516 --> 00:06:53.665
It is a quiet, serene life, and I am grateful to my father for putting me on this

81
00:06:53.805 --> 00:06:55.328
path so long ago.
